y is  it that idea gaining any traction  well I think it has become uh part of  the public debate uh certainly in Europe  but to some extent in the United States  and even in Russia uh you know I note  that the German reporter asked the  question at the press conference between  the chancellor and President Putin today  as to whether a moratorium was a  possibility now neither man answered the  question but I think that's evidence  that it is part of the public debate  today the German Chancellor said that  Ukraine's membership in NATO is not on  the agenda and he said it will not be on  the agenda while he and Vladimir Putin  are in office now Schultz is speaking to  what many diplomats say that former  Soviet republics including Ukraine are  still years away from being ready for  NATO membership am I reading that right  absolutely and I think that almost any  NATO official in any U.S official would  say the same thing we're not talking  about any near-term membership by any  countries of the former Soviet States  and so I think that really is off the  table for an extended period And I do  believe that some agreement on that and  how long that period is is going to be  part of the final solution The  Diplomatic question is how you're going  to formulate that to the satisfaction of  Moscow Brussels and Washington  and all of this is assuming that NATO  will exist in its current form in 20 to  50 years from now I mean that assumption  though it comes with some pitfalls  doesn't it  well certainly nobody knows what the  future is going to be like and that's  one of the reasons why an agreement on a  moratorium might eventually find  approval from Moscow who knows what's  going to happen in 2025 years but I do  think that we will see significant  transformation in NATO under any  circumstances the world has changed uh  the interest of European allies has  changed the United States has to focus  on East Asia as well so we will see  transformation in the way a NATO NATO